    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s44168-025-00332-4",
    "abstract": "Abstract Gridded meteorological datasets\u2014reanalyses and climate simulations\u2014are increasingly central to renewable energy yield assessments, offering complete, physically consistent atmospheric variables required to transform resource data into power output. This review delivers the first holistic synthesis of methodologies and uncertainties across the full modeling chain for both wind and solar energy, from resource characterization to energy yield and bankability metrics. It emphasizes the rising use of long-term, spatially resolved data to improve site selection, financial planning (P50/P90), and climate risk assessment. The review addresses critical modeling steps\u2014vertical extrapolation, air density correction, irradiance decomposition\u2014and quantifies sources of uncertainty inherent in each. By integrating historical and climate-future perspectives, it defines best practices for robust, transparent, and reproducible energy yield assessments.",
    "field": "Renewable energy resource assessment",
    "problem": "The use of gridded climate data for renewable energy assessment lacks a holistic synthesis of methodologies and uncertainties across the full modeling chain\u2014from resource characterization to bankability metrics\u2014hindering robust, transparent, and reproducible energy yield assessments for financial planning and risk management.",
    "method": "This review proposes a framework of best practices for converting gridded climate datasets into wind and solar energy yields, using a complete modeling chain to quantify uncertainties and produce robust, transparent, and reproducible assessments for financial planning and climate risk evaluation.",
    "task": "The task is to synthesize methodologies and uncertainties for assessing renewable wind and solar energy yield using gridded climate datasets, covering the full modeling chain from resource characterization to financial metrics to establish best practices.",
