            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North America",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "The interannual to decadal variability of low-level wind speed in North America from 1950 to 2010 is significantly negatively correlated with the large body of warm water in the Western Pacific, called the Western Hemisphere Warm Pool (WHWP)144.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Similar discrepancies have been observed in Europe146 as the North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) plays a major role in shaping winter wind variability regionally, raising concerns about the financial viability of wind projects and the risk of deterring future investments.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Decadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific basin",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The DPO is a long-term pattern of sea surface temperature variability in the Pacific Ocean, typically fluctuating between warm and cool phases over periods of 10 to 30 years (Fig. 3d). The Decadal Pacific Oscillation influences large-scale atmospheric circulation and climate patterns across the Pacific basin and adjacent continents, including North America.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Decadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North America",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "The DPO is a long-term pattern of sea surface temperature variability in the Pacific Ocean, typically fluctuating between warm and cool phases over periods of 10 to 30 years (Fig. 3d). The Decadal Pacific Oscillation influences large-scale atmospheric circulation and climate patterns across the Pacific basin and adjacent continents, including North America. The low-frequency variability of surface radiation in North America is closely linked to the Decadal Pacific Oscillation (DPO)148.",
